// Comprehensive Deep Reference Book Standard (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora)

export const c12Phy9HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  \n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary &amp; Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF80AB; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Concepts &amp; Key Definitions &bull; Unit IX: Electronic Devices</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF007F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>1. Forbidden Energy Gap (E<sub>g</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Energy separation between the top of the Valence Band (VB) and the bottom of the Conduction Band (CB): E<sub>g</sub> = 1.1 eV for Si, 0.72 eV for Ge [Electron-volt eV / Joule J, [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF007F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>2. Intrinsic Semiconductor:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Extremely pure crystalline semiconductor (Si or Ge) with zero deliberate impurities, where electrical conduction occurs solely due to thermally generated electron-hole pairs (n<sub>e</sub> = n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub>).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF007F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>3. Doping &amp; Extrinsic Semiconductor:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Deliberate addition of a controlled trace impurity (1 part in 10<sup>6</sup>) to an intrinsic semiconductor to drastically increase conductivity (n-type: Pentavalent donor; p-type: Trivalent acceptor).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF007F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>4. Depletion Region &amp; Barrier Potential (V<sub>0</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Narrow region (d &approx; 0.5 - 1 &mu;m) at the p-n metallurgical interface depleted of free mobile charge carriers, containing unneutralized immobile ionized donor (+) and acceptor (-) atoms producing barrier potential V<sub>0</sub> (0.7 V for Si, 0.3 V for Ge).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF007F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>5. Dynamic (AC) Resistance (r<sub>d</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Reciprocal of the slope of the forward I-V characteristic curve: r<sub>d</sub> = &Delta;V / &Delta;I [Ohm &Omega;, [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF007F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>6. Rectification &amp; Efficiency (&eta;):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Process of converting bidirectional alternating current (AC) into unidirectional direct current (DC) using junction diodes: &eta;<sub>max</sub> = 40.6% for Half-Wave, 81.2% for Full-Wave.</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">1. Energy Bands in Conductors, Semiconductors &amp; Insulators</h2>\n<p>In an isolated single atom, electrons occupy discrete, well-defined energy levels. However, in a crystalline solid where billions of atoms are packed together (~10<sup>28</sup> atoms/m<sup>3</sup>), the outermost valence electron wavefunctions overlap strongly. By the Pauli Exclusion Principle, each discrete atomic level splits into a dense continuum of closely spaced energy levels called an <b>Energy Band</b>.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>• <b>Valence Band (VB):</b> The lower energy band occupied by valence electrons involved in covalent bonding. It is completely filled at 0 K and never conducts current on its own without vacancies.</li>\n    <li>• <b>Conduction Band (CB):</b> The higher energy band containing quasi-free electrons that move freely through the crystal lattice under an applied electric field.</li>\n    <li>• <b>Forbidden Energy Gap (E<sub>g</sub>):</b> The energy separation between the top of VB and bottom of CB where no electron quantum states can exist.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 1: Energy Bands in Conductors, Semiconductors &amp; Insulators\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.1:</b> Energy Bands in Solids &ndash; (a) Conductor: overlapping VB and CB (E<sub>g</sub> = 0), (b) Semiconductor: small forbidden band gap (E<sub>g</sub> &approx; 1 eV), (c) Insulator: wide forbidden band gap (E<sub>g</sub> &gt; 3 eV).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 10px;\">Classification of Solids Based on Energy Band Structure:</b>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 10px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00E5FF;\">1. Conductors (Metals):</b><br>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Valence band and conduction band <b>overlap directly (E<sub>g</sub> = 0)</b>, or CB is partially filled. Massive pool of free conduction electrons (&sigma; ~ 10<sup>2</sup> - 10<sup>8</sup> S/m). Resistance increases with temperature.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #76FF03; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">2. Semiconductors (Si, Ge):</b><br>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Filled VB and empty CB at 0 K separated by a <b>small band gap (E<sub>g</sub> &lt; 3 eV)</b>: E<sub>g</sub>(Si) = 1.1 eV, E<sub>g</sub>(Ge) = 0.72 eV. Thermal energy easily excites electrons across E<sub>g</sub>. Negative temperature coefficient of resistance.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">3. Insulators (Diamond, Glass):</b><br>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Filled VB and completely empty CB separated by a <b>huge band gap (E<sub>g</sub> &gt; 3 eV)</b>: E<sub>g</sub>(Diamond) = 5.4 eV. Even large electric fields cannot impart enough energy to excite electrons into CB.</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #FF80AB; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF80AB; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Energy Bands as a 2-Story Building with Ceiling Height</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Imagine a building where workers are on the 1st floor (Valence Band) and the executive workstations are on the 2nd floor (Conduction Band). In metals, the two floors are connected by an open escalator with zero gap (E<sub>g</sub> = 0). In semiconductors, the ceiling height is a low 1-meter hop (E<sub>g</sub> &approx; 1 eV) &ndash; a little thermal boost lets workers leap up effortlessly! In insulators, the ceiling is a 10-meter cliff (E<sub>g</sub> &gt; 3 eV) &ndash; nobody can jump that high!</div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">2. Intrinsic and Extrinsic Semiconductors (p-Type and n-Type)</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Intrinsic Semiconductors (Pure Si &amp; Ge Lattice)</h3>\n<p>Silicon (Z = 14) and Germanium (Z = 32) are tetravalent elements with 4 valence electrons. In a pure crystal, each atom shares its 4 valence electrons with 4 neighboring atoms, forming 4 strong covalent bonds. At absolute zero (T = 0 K), all covalent bonds are completely intact and valence electrons are tightly bound; hence, an intrinsic semiconductor acts as a <b>perfect insulator at 0 K</b>.</p>\n  <p>At room temperature (T &gt; 0 K), thermal vibrations break a small fraction of covalent bonds. An electron escaping a covalent bond enters the conduction band as a <b>free electron</b>, leaving behind an electron vacancy with effective positive charge called a <b>Hole</b> in the valence band.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 4px solid #76FF03; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>n<sub>e</sub> = n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; and &nbsp;&nbsp; Total Current: I = I<sub>e</sub> + I<sub>h</sub></b></span>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 2: Intrinsic Semiconductor (Pure Silicon Crystal Lattice)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.2:</b> Intrinsic Semiconductor &ndash; Pure Silicon crystalline lattice showing covalent bonds and thermal creation of equal numbers of free electrons and holes (n<sub>e</sub> = n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub>).</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Extrinsic Semiconductors (Doping, n-Type &amp; p-Type)</h3>\n<p>Pure semiconductors have very low electrical conductivity at room temperature (~1.5 &times; 10<sup>16</sup> m<sup>-3</sup> carriers in Si). To make them useful in electronic devices, deliberate impurities are added through <b>Doping</b> (typically 1 impurity atom per 10<sup>6</sup> host atoms).</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 3: Extrinsic Semiconductors (P-Type &amp; N-Type)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.3:</b> Extrinsic Semiconductors &ndash; (a) P-type doped with trivalent Boron creating mobile holes as majority carriers; (b) N-type doped with pentavalent Phosphorus donating extra free electrons as majority carriers.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1.2px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">⚡ 1. n-Type Semiconductor (Donor Doped):</b>\n      <p style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Doped with <b>Pentavalent impurity</b> (P, As, Sb). 4 of the 5 valence electrons form covalent bonds, while the 5th electron is loosely bound (ionization energy ~0.05 eV for Si) and easily enters CB at room temperature.</p>\n      <ul style=\"margin: 0 0 0 16px; padding: 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n        <li>• <b>Majority Carriers:</b> Electrons (n<sub>e</sub> &approx; N<sub>d</sub> &gg; n<sub>h</sub>).</li>\n        <li>• <b>Minority Carriers:</b> Thermally generated holes.</li>\n        <li>• <b>Donor Energy Level (E<sub>d</sub>):</b> Lies just below CB bottom (~0.05 eV).</li>\n        <li>• <b>Electrical Charge:</b> Perfectly electrically neutral (bulk crystal has zero net charge).</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1.2px solid #FF80AB; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF80AB; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">🌸 2. p-Type Semiconductor (Acceptor Doped):</b>\n      <p style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Doped with <b>Trivalent impurity</b> (B, Al, In, Ga). 3 valence electrons form bonds with 3 neighbors; the 4th bond lacks an electron, creating a vacancy (hole) that easily accepts an electron from a neighboring bond.</p>\n      <ul style=\"margin: 0 0 0 16px; padding: 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n        <li>• <b>Majority Carriers:</b> Holes (n<sub>h</sub> &approx; N<sub>a</sub> &gg; n<sub>e</sub>).</li>\n        <li>• <b>Minority Carriers:</b> Thermally generated electrons.</li>\n        <li>• <b>Acceptor Energy Level (E<sub>a</sub>):</b> Lies just above VB top (~0.05 eV).</li>\n        <li>• <b>Electrical Charge:</b> Perfectly electrically neutral (bulk crystal has zero net charge).</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Law of Mass Action for Semiconductors (n_e &bull; n_h = n_i²)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">In thermal equilibrium, the product of free electron concentration (n_e) and hole concentration (n_h) is a constant independent of doping levels and depends only on temperature and band gap: n_e &bull; n_h = n_i².</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">m⁻⁶</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M⁰ L⁻⁶ T⁰]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">3. p-n Junction Formation, Depletion Region &amp; Barrier Potential</h2>\n<p>When a p-type semiconductor is intimately joined with an n-type semiconductor on an atomic scale (metallurgical junction), two simultaneous physical processes take place:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>1. <b>Diffusion:</b> High concentration of holes in p-side causes holes to diffuse across the junction into n-side. High concentration of electrons in n-side causes electrons to diffuse into p-side. This initial motion constitutes <b>Diffusion Current (I<sub>diff</sub>)</b> from p to n.</li>\n    <li>2. <b>Depletion Layer Formation:</b> As electrons cross from n to p, they leave behind unneutralized immobile positive donor ions (+). As holes cross from p to n, they leave behind unneutralized immobile negative acceptor ions (-). This region near the junction devoid of free mobile carriers is the <b>Depletion Region</b> (thickness d &approx; 0.5 - 1 &mu;m).</li>\n    <li>3. <b>Internal Electric Field &amp; Drift Current:</b> The positive donor ions on the n-side and negative acceptor ions on the p-side establish a strong internal electric field <b>E&#8407;<sub>i</sub> ~ 10<sup>5</sup> V/m</b> pointing from n to p. This field sweeps minority carriers across the junction (minority holes from n to p, minority electrons from p to n), creating <b>Drift Current (I<sub>drift</sub>)</b> pointing from n to p.</li>\n    <li>4. <b>Dynamic Equilibrium &amp; Barrier Potential (V<sub>0</sub>):</b> As the depletion layer widens, drift current increases until it exactly balances diffusion current: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>I<sub>diff</sub> = I<sub>drift</sub> (Net Current = 0)</b></span>. The potential difference across the depletion layer at equilibrium is called the <b>Barrier Potential (V<sub>0</sub> &approx; 0.7 V for Si, 0.3 V for Ge)</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 4: Formation of p-n Junction &amp; Depletion Region\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.4:</b> p-n Junction Structure &ndash; Diffusion of mobile carriers leaves behind unneutralized fixed acceptor ions (-) in p-side and donor ions (+) in n-side, forming a central Depletion Region with built-in barrier potential.</p>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">4. Semiconductor Diode &amp; I-V Characteristics in Forward &amp; Reverse Bias</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 5: Semiconductor Diode Symbol &amp; Structure\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.5:</b> Semiconductor Diode &ndash; Circuit schematic symbol (Anode P +, Cathode N -) and physical two-terminal semiconductor junction block.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 6: Basic Diode Circuit Setup\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.6:</b> Diode Circuit &ndash; When the Anode (A) is connected to the positive terminal of the voltage source through resistor R, the diode conducts forward current I.</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin-top: 25px;\">(i) Forward Biasing of a Junction Diode</h3>\n<p>When the positive terminal of an external DC source is connected to the <b>p-side</b> and negative terminal to the <b>n-side</b>:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>• The applied forward voltage V opposes the internal barrier potential V<sub>0</sub>, reducing the effective barrier to <b>(V<sub>0</sub> - V)</b>.</li>\n    <li>• The depletion layer width <b>decreases (becomes narrow)</b>.</li>\n    <li>• When applied voltage exceeds the <b>Knee Voltage / Cut-in Voltage (V<sub>k</sub> &approx; 0.7 V for Si, 0.3 V for Ge)</b>, majority carriers overcome the barrier in huge numbers, causing current to rise exponentially (measured in milliamperes <b>mA</b>).</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 7: Forward Biased p-n Junction Diode\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.7:</b> Forward Bias Operation &ndash; P-side connected to positive terminal, N-side to negative; depletion region narrows and large forward current I flows.</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Reverse Biasing of a Junction Diode</h3>\n<p>When the positive terminal of an external DC source is connected to the <b>n-side</b> and negative terminal to the <b>p-side</b>:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>• The applied reverse voltage V aids the internal barrier potential, increasing effective barrier height to <b>(V<sub>0</sub> + V)</b>.</li>\n    <li>• The depletion layer width <b>increases (becomes wide)</b>.</li>\n    <li>• Majority carriers are pulled away from the junction; hence, majority conduction is completely blocked.</li>\n    <li>• A tiny <b>Reverse Saturation Current (I<sub>s</sub> / I<sub>0</sub>)</b> of order <b>&mu;A (for Ge) or nA (for Si)</b> flows exclusively due to thermally generated minority carriers across the junction.</li>\n    <li>• At sufficiently high reverse voltage (Breakdown Voltage V<sub>BR</sub>), covalent bonds rupture violently, causing current to surge rapidly.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 8: Reverse Biased p-n Junction Diode\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.8:</b> Reverse Bias Operation &ndash; P-side connected to negative terminal, N-side to positive; depletion region widens and majority current is blocked (I &approx; 0 except tiny reverse saturation current).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 9: Complete I-V Characteristics Curve of Diode\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.9:</b> I-V Characteristics Curve &ndash; Forward region with Knee Voltage (0.7 V Si, 0.3 V Ge) and exponential mA current rise; Reverse region with tiny saturation current (I<sub>s</sub>) leading to Breakdown Voltage (V<sub>BR</sub>).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Dynamic (AC) Resistance of Diode from I-V Curve</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Definition of Dynamic Resistance):</b><br>\n    Since the diode I-V curve is non-linear (does not obey Ohm's law V = IR with constant R), we define dynamic (AC) resistance as the ratio of a small change in voltage (&Delta;V) to the resulting small change in current (&Delta;I):<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>r<sub>d</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;V</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;I</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Slope of I-V curve</span></span></b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Forward vs Reverse Resistance Magnitude):</b><br>\n    • <b>Forward Dynamic Resistance (R<sub>F</sub>):</b> In forward bias beyond knee voltage, &Delta;I is large (~10 - 50 mA) for small &Delta;V (~0.1 V), giving low resistance: <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>R<sub>F</sub> &approx; 1 - 25 &Omega;</b></span>.<br>\n    • <b>Reverse Dynamic Resistance (R<sub>R</sub>):</b> In reverse bias below breakdown, &Delta;I is virtually zero (~nA), giving enormous resistance: <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>R<sub>R</sub> &approx; 10<sup>6</sup> &Omega; = 1 M&Omega;</b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Ideal Diode Model):</b><br>\n    In ideal circuit calculations: Forward biased diode acts as a perfect closed switch (R<sub>F</sub> = 0, V<sub>drop</sub> = 0); Reverse biased diode acts as a perfect open switch (R<sub>R</sub> = &infin;, I = 0).\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">5. Application of Junction Diode as a Rectifier</h2>\n<p>A junction diode conducts current with low resistance when forward biased and blocks current with high resistance when reverse biased. This <b>unidirectional conduction property</b> allows it to act as a <b>Rectifier</b> &ndash; converting alternating AC voltage into pulsating DC voltage.</p>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Half-Wave Rectifier</h3>\n<p>In a half-wave rectifier, a single diode D is connected in series with the secondary winding of a step-down transformer and load resistor R<sub>L</sub>.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>• <b>During Positive Half Cycle:</b> Upper terminal of secondary is positive relative to lower terminal. Diode D is forward biased and conducts current through R<sub>L</sub> from top to bottom.</li>\n    <li>• <b>During Negative Half Cycle:</b> Upper terminal becomes negative. Diode D is reverse biased and conducts no current (I = 0).</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 10: Diode as Half-Wave Rectifier\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.10:</b> Half-Wave Rectifier Circuit &amp; Waveforms &ndash; Diode conducts during positive half cycles only, producing pulsating DC with maximum efficiency &eta;<sub>max</sub> = 40.6% and ripple frequency f<sub>out</sub> = f<sub>in</sub>.</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Full-Wave Center-Tapped Rectifier</h3>\n<p>In a full-wave center-tapped rectifier, two diodes (D<sub>1</sub> and D<sub>2</sub>) are connected to the opposite ends of a center-tapped secondary winding, with load resistor R<sub>L</sub> connected between the center tap CT and the common cathode junction.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>• <b>During Positive Half Cycle:</b> Upper terminal is positive and lower is negative with respect to center tap CT. Diode <b>D<sub>1</sub> is forward biased</b> and conducts, while <b>D<sub>2</sub> is reverse biased</b>. Current flows through R<sub>L</sub> from right to left.</li>\n    <li>• <b>During Negative Half Cycle:</b> Upper terminal is negative and lower is positive with respect to CT. Diode <b>D<sub>2</sub> is forward biased</b> and conducts, while <b>D<sub>1</sub> is reverse biased</b>. Current flows through R<sub>L</sub> in the <b>exact same direction</b>!</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 11: Diode as Full-Wave Rectifier (Center Tap)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.11:</b> Full-Wave Center-Tapped Rectifier &ndash; Diodes conduct in alternate half cycles, utilizing both AC half-waves to deliver smoother pulsating DC with &eta;<sub>max</sub> = 81.2% and ripple frequency f<sub>out</sub> = 2f<sub>in</sub>.</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin-top: 25px;\">(iii) Full-Wave Bridge Rectifier &amp; Capacitor Filter Circuit</h3>\n<p>A <b>Bridge Rectifier</b> uses four diodes (D<sub>1</sub>, D<sub>2</sub>, D<sub>3</sub>, D<sub>4</sub>) connected in a closed bridge loop. During positive half cycles, diodes D<sub>1</sub> and D<sub>2</sub> conduct in series. During negative half cycles, diodes D<sub>3</sub> and D<sub>4</sub> conduct in series. It provides full-wave rectification without requiring a bulky center-tapped transformer.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 12: Diode as Full-Wave Bridge Rectifier\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 9.12:</b> Full-Wave Bridge Rectifier &ndash; Four-diode bridge network utilizing both AC half cycles with superior efficiency and eliminating the need for a center-tapped transformer.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Rectification Efficiency (&eta;) and Ripple Factor (&gamma;) Derivation</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Output DC Power &amp; Input AC Power):</b><br>\n    Let secondary AC voltage be v = V<sub>m</sub> sin(&omega;t), and let diode forward resistance be r<sub>f</sub> and load be R<sub>L</sub>.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Half-Wave Rectifier):</b><br>\n    • DC Current: I<sub>dc</sub> = I<sub>m</sub> / &pi; &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; P<sub>dc</sub> = (I<sub>m</sub> / &pi;)<sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub>.<br>\n    • RMS Current: I<sub>rms</sub> = I<sub>m</sub> / 2 &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; P<sub>ac</sub> = (I<sub>m</sub> / 2)<sup>2</sup> (r<sub>f</sub> + R<sub>L</sub>).<br>\n    • Efficiency: &eta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sub>dc</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sub>ac</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(I<sub>m</sub>/&pi;)<sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(I<sub>m</sub>/2)<sup>2</sup> (r<sub>f</sub> + R<sub>L</sub>)</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi;<sup>2</sup></span></span> [ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R<sub>L</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>f</sub> + R<sub>L</sub></span></span> ] &approx; <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>40.6%</b></span> (for r<sub>f</sub> &Lt; R<sub>L</sub>).<br>\n    • Ripple Frequency: <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>f<sub>ripple</sub> = f<sub>in</sub> = 50 Hz</b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Full-Wave Rectifier):</b><br>\n    • DC Current: I<sub>dc</sub> = 2 I<sub>m</sub> / &pi; &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; P<sub>dc</sub> = (2 I<sub>m</sub> / &pi;)<sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub>.<br>\n    • RMS Current: I<sub>rms</sub> = I<sub>m</sub> / &radic;2 &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; P<sub>ac</sub> = (I<sub>m</sub> / &radic;2)<sup>2</sup> (r<sub>f</sub> + R<sub>L</sub>).<br>\n    • Efficiency: &eta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sub>dc</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sub>ac</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(2 I<sub>m</sub>/&pi;)<sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(I<sub>m</sub>/&radic;2)<sup>2</sup> (r<sub>f</sub> + R<sub>L</sub>)</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi;<sup>2</sup></span></span> [ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R<sub>L</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>f</sub> + R<sub>L</sub></span></span> ] &approx; <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>81.2%</b></span> (for r<sub>f</sub> &Lt; R<sub>L</sub>).<br>\n    • Ripple Frequency: <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>f<sub>ripple</sub> = 2 f<sub>in</sub> = 100 Hz</b></span> (for 50 Hz mains).\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-top: 35px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center;\">⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet &bull; Unit IX: Electronic Devices</h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin-top: 14px; font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Mass Action Law:</b> n<sub>e</sub> &bull; n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub>²</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Intrinsic Conductivity:</b> &sigma; = e (n<sub>e</sub> &mu;<sub>e</sub> + n<sub>h</sub> &mu;<sub>h</sub>)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Dynamic Resistance:</b> r<sub>d</sub> = &Delta;V / &Delta;I</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Knee Voltages:</b> V<sub>k</sub>(Si) = 0.7 V &nbsp;|&nbsp; V<sub>k</sub>(Ge) = 0.3 V</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Half-Wave Efficiency:</b> &eta;<sub>max</sub> = 40.6% &nbsp;|&nbsp; f<sub>out</sub> = f<sub>in</sub></div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Full-Wave Efficiency:</b> &eta;<sub>max</sub> = 81.2% &nbsp;|&nbsp; f<sub>out</sub> = 2 f<sub>in</sub></div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Half-Wave DC Voltage:</b> V<sub>dc</sub> = V<sub>m</sub> / &pi;</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Full-Wave DC Voltage:</b> V<sub>dc</sub> = 2 V<sub>m</sub> / &pi;</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Band Gaps:</b> E<sub>g</sub>(Si) = 1.1 eV &nbsp;|&nbsp; E<sub>g</sub>(Ge) = 0.72 eV</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Barrier Electric Field:</b> E = V<sub>0</sub> / d &approx; 10⁵ V/m</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

export const c12Phy9HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 4px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">🎯 Complete 100 Board Solutions &bull; Unit IX: Electronic Devices</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF80AB; margin: 0; font-size: 14px; text-align: center !important;\">Section A (Q1–Q40: 1 Mark VSA) &bull; Section B (Q41–Q80: 2 &amp; 3 Marks SA) &bull; Section C (Q81–Q100: 5 Marks LA)</p>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">SECTION A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark Each &bull; Q1 to Q40)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q1: What is the value of the forbidden energy gap in conductors (metals)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In conductors, the forbidden energy gap is zero: <b>E<sub>g</sub> = 0 eV</b> (valence band and conduction band overlap).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q2: What is the forbidden energy gap for Silicon and Germanium at room temperature (300 K)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      For Silicon: <b>E<sub>g</sub> = 1.1 eV</b>; For Germanium: <b>E<sub>g</sub> = 0.72 eV &approx; 0.7 eV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q3: What is the value of the forbidden energy gap in Diamond (insulator)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In Diamond, the forbidden energy gap is approximately <b>E<sub>g</sub> &approx; 5.4 eV</b> (well above 3 eV).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q4: How does the electrical conductivity of an intrinsic semiconductor change with increase in temperature?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Conductivity <b>increases exponentially with temperature</b> because thermal energy breaks more covalent bonds, generating more electron-hole pairs.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q5: What is a hole in a semiconductor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A hole is an unoccupied electron vacancy in the valence band created when an electron jumps to the conduction band, acting as an effective positive charge of magnitude <b>+e = +1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q6: Name two trivalent dopants and two pentavalent dopants used in semiconductors.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • <b>Trivalent (Acceptors for p-type):</b> Boron (B), Aluminum (Al), Indium (In).<br>• <b>Pentavalent (Donors for n-type):</b> Phosphorus (P), Arsenic (As), Antimony (Sb).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q7: State the Mass Action Law for a semiconductor in thermal equilibrium.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The product of free electron concentration (n<sub>e</sub>) and hole concentration (n<sub>h</sub>) is constant: <b>n<sub>e</sub> &times; n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub><sup>2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q8: Is an n-type semiconductor positively charged, negatively charged, or neutral?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      An n-type semiconductor is <b>strictly electrically neutral</b> because every free electron is balanced by a stationary positive donor ion core.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q9: What are the majority and minority charge carriers in a p-type semiconductor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In a p-type semiconductor, <b>Holes are majority carriers</b> (n<sub>h</sub> &gt;&gt; n<sub>e</sub>) and <b>Electrons are minority carriers</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q10: What is the thickness of the depletion layer in a typical silicon p-n junction?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The depletion layer thickness is of the order of <b>0.5 &mu;m to 1 &mu;m (10<sup>-6</sup> m)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q11: What is the value of the barrier potential for a Silicon diode and a Germanium diode?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Barrier potential: <b>V<sub>0</sub> &approx; 0.7 V for Silicon</b> and <b>V<sub>0</sub> &approx; 0.3 V for Germanium</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q12: How does the width of the depletion layer change in forward bias?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In forward bias, the depletion layer width <b>decreases (narrows)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q13: How does the width of the depletion layer change in reverse bias?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In reverse bias, the depletion layer width <b>increases (widens)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q14: What causes diffusion current in a p-n junction?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Diffusion current is caused by the <b>concentration gradient</b> of majority charge carriers diffusing across the junction (electrons from n &rarr; p, holes from p &rarr; n).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q15: What causes drift current in a p-n junction?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Drift current is caused by the <b>built-in internal electric field (&vec;E<sub>i</sub>)</b> sweeping minority carriers across the depletion layer (holes from n &rarr; p, electrons from p &rarr; n).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q16: What is knee voltage (cut-in voltage) of a diode?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Knee voltage (V<sub>k</sub>) is the forward bias voltage beyond which the diode current begins to increase sharply and exponentially.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q17: What is the order of forward current and reverse current in a junction diode?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Forward current is in <b>milliamperes (mA)</b>; Reverse saturation current is in <b>microamperes (&mu;A) for Ge and nanoamperes (nA) for Si</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q18: Define dynamic resistance of a diode.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Dynamic resistance (r<sub>d</sub>) is the ratio of small change in voltage to corresponding small change in current: <b>r<sub>d</sub> = &Delta;V / &Delta;I</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q19: What is the ideal forward and reverse resistance of a diode?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Ideal forward resistance is <b>R<sub>F</sub> = 0 &Omega; (short circuit)</b>; Ideal reverse resistance is <b>R<sub>R</sub> = &infin; (open circuit)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q20: What is the primary function of a rectifier?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A rectifier converts <b>alternating current (AC) into pulsating direct current (DC)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q21: What is the maximum theoretical efficiency of a half-wave rectifier?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The maximum theoretical efficiency of a half-wave rectifier is <b>&eta;<sub>max</sub> = 40.6%</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q22: What is the maximum theoretical efficiency of a full-wave rectifier?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The maximum theoretical efficiency of a full-wave rectifier is <b>&eta;<sub>max</sub> = 81.2%</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q23: If the input AC frequency to a half-wave rectifier is 50 Hz, what is the output ripple frequency?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The output ripple frequency is <b>f<sub>out</sub> = f<sub>in</sub> = 50 Hz</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q24: If the input AC frequency to a full-wave rectifier is 50 Hz, what is the output ripple frequency?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The output ripple frequency is <b>f<sub>out</sub> = 2 f<sub>in</sub> = 2 &times; 50 = 100 Hz</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q25: How many diodes are used in a center-tapped full-wave rectifier?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>2 diodes</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q26: How many diodes are used in a bridge full-wave rectifier?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>4 diodes</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q27: Why is Carbon an insulator while Silicon and Germanium are semiconductors even though all belong to Group 14?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Carbon has a very small atomic size and tightly bound electrons resulting in a large band gap <b>E<sub>g</sub>(C) &approx; 5.4 eV</b> (insulator), whereas larger Si and Ge have smaller gaps <b>E<sub>g</sub>(Si) = 1.1 eV, E<sub>g</sub>(Ge) = 0.7 eV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q28: Why is Silicon preferred over Germanium for manufacturing semiconductor diodes?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Silicon has a larger band gap (1.1 eV vs 0.7 eV), giving it a much <b>smaller reverse saturation current (nA vs &mu;A)</b> and higher thermal stability at elevated operating temperatures.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q29: What is the role of a capacitor filter in a rectifier circuit?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A capacitor filter connected across the load resistor charges to peak voltage and discharges slowly, <b>smoothing out pulsating DC ripples into a nearly steady DC voltage</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q30: Where does the donor energy level lie in an n-type semiconductor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The donor energy level (E<sub>d</sub>) lies in the forbidden gap <b>just below the conduction band</b> (~0.05 eV below CB in Si).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q31: Where does the acceptor energy level lie in a p-type semiconductor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The acceptor energy level (E<sub>a</sub>) lies in the forbidden gap <b>just above the valence band</b> (~0.05 eV above VB in Si).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q32: Can we measure the potential barrier of a p-n junction by simply connecting a voltmeter across its terminals? Why?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>No.</b> Because the contact potential developed at the metal-semiconductor connecting leads exactly balances the barrier potential, yielding zero net external voltage.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q33: Why is mobility of electrons greater than mobility of holes in semiconductors?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Electrons move freely in the conduction band in empty states, while holes move in the valence band via a step-by-step bound covalent bond replacement mechanism, making <b>&mu;<sub>e</sub> &gt; &mu;<sub>h</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q34: What happens to the resistance of a semiconductor as temperature increases?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Its electrical resistance <b>decreases</b> (it has a negative temperature coefficient of resistance, &alpha; &lt; 0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q35: What is the net charge on a p-type semiconductor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The net charge is <b>zero (strictly electrically neutral)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q36: What is the direction of the internal electric field in the depletion layer of a p-n junction?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      From <b>n-side (positive donor ions) to p-side (negative acceptor ions)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q37: What happens to the barrier potential of a p-n junction when temperature increases?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Barrier potential <b>decreases</b> (~2 mV per &deg;C rise) because higher temperature increases intrinsic carrier generation and narrows the band gap.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q38: What is the full-wave rectifier output DC voltage in terms of peak voltage V_m?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Output DC voltage is: <b>V<sub>dc</sub> = 2 V<sub>m</sub> / &pi; &approx; 0.636 V<sub>m</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q39: What is the half-wave rectifier output DC voltage in terms of peak voltage V_m?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Output DC voltage is: <b>V<sub>dc</sub> = V<sub>m</sub> / &pi; &approx; 0.318 V<sub>m</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q40: Name the semiconductor device used to convert solar light energy directly into electrical energy.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Solar Cell (Photovoltaic Diode)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">SECTION B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 &amp; 3 Marks Each &bull; Q41 to Q80)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q41: A pure silicon crystal has 5 x 10²⁸ atoms/m3. It is doped with 1 ppm concentration of Arsenic (pentavalent). If n_i = 1.5 x 10¹⁶ m-3, calculate the concentration of electrons and holes.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Doping concentration N<sub>D</sub> = 1 ppm of (5 &times; 10<sup>28</sup>) = 5 &times; 10<sup>28</sup> / 10<sup>6</sup> = 5 &times; 10<sup>22</sup> m<sup>-3</sup>.<br>Since it is n-type: n<sub>e</sub> &approx; N<sub>D</sub> = <b>5 &times; 10<sup>22</sup> m<sup>-3</sup></b>.<br>By Mass Action Law: n<sub>e</sub> &times; n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub><sup>2</sup> &rArr; n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub><sup>2</sup> / n<sub>e</sub>.<br>n<sub>h</sub> = (1.5 &times; 10<sup>16</sup>)<sup>2</sup> / (5 &times; 10<sup>22</sup>) = (2.25 &times; 10<sup>32</sup>) / (5 &times; 10<sup>22</sup>) = <b>4.5 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> m<sup>-3</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q42: In an intrinsic semiconductor, the energy gap E_g is 1.2 eV. Find the maximum wavelength of light that can create an electron-hole pair.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Formula: E<sub>g</sub> = h c / &lambda;<sub>max</sub> &rArr; &lambda;<sub>max</sub> = h c / E<sub>g</sub>.<br>&lambda;<sub>max</sub> = (6.63 &times; 10<sup>-34</sup> &times; 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (1.2 &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> J) = (1.989 &times; 10<sup>-25</sup>) / (1.92 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup>) = <b>1.036 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> m = 1036 nm = 1.036 &mu;m</b> (Infrared).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q43: The V-I characteristic of a silicon diode shows that forward current changes from 10 mA to 20 mA when forward voltage changes from 0.7 V to 0.75 V. Calculate the dynamic forward resistance of the diode.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: &Delta;V = 0.75 - 0.70 = 0.05 V, &Delta;I = 20 mA - 10 mA = 10 mA = 10 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> A.<br>Dynamic Resistance: <b>r<sub>d</sub> = &Delta;V / &Delta;I = 0.05 / (10 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) = 5 &Omega;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q44: In a p-n junction diode, the current in reverse bias is 20 &mu;A at -5 V and 25 &mu;A at -10 V. Calculate the dynamic reverse resistance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: &Delta;V = |-10 - (-5)| = 5 V, &Delta;I = 25 &mu;A - 20 &mu;A = 5 &mu;A = 5 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> A.<br>Reverse Dynamic Resistance: <b>r<sub>r</sub> = &Delta;V / &Delta;I = 5 / (5 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>) = 10<sup>6</sup> &Omega; = 1 M&Omega;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q45: A silicon diode of barrier potential 0.7 V is connected in series with a resistor R = 200 &Omega; across a 5 V DC supply in forward bias. Calculate: (i) the diode current, (ii) power dissipated in resistor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: V<sub>supply</sub> = 5 V, V<sub>0</sub> = 0.7 V, R = 200 &Omega;.<br>(i) Effective voltage across resistor: V<sub>R</sub> = V<sub>supply</sub> - V<sub>0</sub> = 5 - 0.7 = 4.3 V.<br>Diode current: <b>I = V<sub>R</sub> / R = 4.3 / 200 = 0.0215 A = 21.5 mA</b>.<br>(ii) Power dissipated in R: <b>P = I<sup>2</sup> R = (0.0215)<sup>2</sup> &times; 200 = 0.09245 W &approx; 92.5 mW</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q46: Distinguish between intrinsic and extrinsic semiconductors with four points of difference.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Purity:</b> Intrinsic is extremely pure (Si/Ge); Extrinsic is doped with trace trivalent/pentavalent impurities.<br>2. <b>Carrier Densities:</b> Intrinsic has equal electrons and holes (n<sub>e</sub> = n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub>); Extrinsic has unequal densities (n<sub>e</sub> &gt;&gt; n<sub>h</sub> in n-type, n<sub>h</sub> &gt;&gt; n<sub>e</sub> in p-type).<br>3. <b>Conductivity:</b> Intrinsic has very low electrical conductivity; Extrinsic has high, controllable conductivity.<br>4. <b>Temperature Dependence:</b> Intrinsic conductivity depends only on temperature; Extrinsic conductivity depends primarily on dopant concentration.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q47: Distinguish between an n-type semiconductor and a p-type semiconductor with three comparison criteria.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Dopant:</b> n-type is doped with Pentavalent atoms (P, As, Sb - Donors); p-type is doped with Trivalent atoms (B, Al, Ga - Acceptors).<br>2. <b>Majority Carriers:</b> n-type majority carriers are Free Electrons; p-type majority carriers are Holes.<br>3. <b>Impurity Energy Level:</b> Donor level E<sub>d</sub> lies just below conduction band; Acceptor level E<sub>a</sub> lies just above valence band.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q48: Explain the formation of depletion region and barrier potential in a p-n junction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • When p and n materials meet, electrons diffuse from n &rarr; p and holes from p &rarr; n due to concentration gradients.<br>• Recombination near interface uncovers immobile positive donor ions on n-side and negative acceptor ions on p-side, forming a charge-free <b>Depletion Layer</b> (thickness ~0.5 - 1 &mu;m).<br>• The space charge creates an internal electric field &vec;E<sub>i</sub> directed from n &rarr; p. This develops a <b>Barrier Potential V<sub>0</sub> (~0.7 V for Si)</b> which opposes further majority diffusion until equilibrium (I<sub>diff</sub> = I<sub>drift</sub>) is achieved.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q49: Draw the circuit diagram for studying the forward and reverse bias characteristics of a p-n junction diode. Explain the knee voltage.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The circuit consists of a variable DC source connected across the diode with a milliammeter/microammeter and voltmeter. In forward bias, p-side is connected to positive terminal. Below the <b>Knee Voltage (V<sub>k</sub> &approx; 0.7 V for Si)</b>, forward current is negligibly small as the applied voltage is less than the barrier potential. Once V &gt; V<sub>k</sub>, the barrier is eliminated and current rises exponentially.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q50: Explain why a p-n junction diode offers very low resistance in forward bias and very high resistance in reverse bias.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • <b>Forward Bias:</b> Applied positive voltage on p-side opposes the internal barrier field &vec;E<sub>i</sub>, shrinking the depletion width and lowering barrier height to (V<sub>0</sub> - V). Majority carriers easily cross the junction, producing high current and very low resistance (R<sub>F</sub> ~ 10 &Omega;).<br>• <b>Reverse Bias:</b> Applied field aids &vec;E<sub>i</sub>, widening the depletion region and elevating barrier height to (V<sub>0</sub> + V), blocking all majority diffusion and permitting only tiny minority drift current, resulting in huge resistance (R<sub>R</sub> ~ 1 M&Omega;).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q51: Explain the working of a center-tapped full-wave rectifier with a neat circuit diagram. State its output frequency if input is 50 Hz.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • In a center-tapped rectifier, two diodes D<sub>1</sub> and D<sub>2</sub> are connected across the secondary of a center-tapped transformer with load resistor R<sub>L</sub> connected between the center tap and common diode cathode junction.<br>• During positive half cycle: D<sub>1</sub> is forward biased (conducts) while D<sub>2</sub> is reverse biased (OFF).<br>• During negative half cycle: D<sub>2</sub> is forward biased (conducts) while D<sub>1</sub> is reverse biased (OFF).<br>• Current flows through R<sub>L</sub> in the exact same direction during both half cycles, producing full-wave DC.<br>• If input frequency is 50 Hz, <b>output ripple frequency is 2 &times; 50 = 100 Hz</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q52: A half-wave rectifier is supplied with 220 V AC at 50 Hz through a step-down transformer of turns ratio 10:1. Assuming ideal diode, find: (i) peak output voltage, (ii) DC output voltage.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Secondary RMS voltage: V<sub>rms</sub> = 220 / 10 = 22 V.<br>(i) Peak output voltage: <b>V<sub>m</sub> = &radic;2 &times; V<sub>rms</sub> = 1.414 &times; 22 = 31.11 V</b>.<br>(ii) DC output voltage: <b>V<sub>dc</sub> = V<sub>m</sub> / &pi; = 31.11 / 3.1416 = 9.90 V</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q53: For a full-wave rectifier supplied with peak secondary voltage V_m = 20 V, calculate: (i) DC output voltage, (ii) RMS output voltage.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: V<sub>m</sub> = 20 V.<br>(i) DC output voltage: <b>V<sub>dc</sub> = 2 V<sub>m</sub> / &pi; = (2 &times; 20) / 3.1416 = 40 / 3.1416 = 12.73 V</b>.<br>(ii) RMS output voltage: <b>V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>m</sub> / &radic;2 = 20 / 1.414 = 14.14 V</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q54: Explain the working of a capacitor filter connected across the output of a full-wave rectifier.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A capacitor C connected in parallel with load resistor R<sub>L</sub> charges to peak voltage V<sub>m</sub> during the conduction cycle of the diodes. When the rectifier voltage drops, the capacitor discharges slowly through R<sub>L</sub> (time constant &tau; = R<sub>L</sub>C is large), maintaining the output voltage near peak value and replacing sharp pulsating ripples with smooth DC.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q55: Why does the reverse saturation current of a p-n junction diode remain almost constant with increasing reverse voltage up to breakdown?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The reverse saturation current is carried entirely by <b>minority charge carriers</b> thermally generated in the depletion region and neutral bulk. Since thermal generation rate depends strictly on temperature (not on applied reverse voltage), all available minority carriers are swept across even at low reverse voltage, causing current to saturate.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q56: Calculate the electrical conductivity of an intrinsic semiconductor at room temperature if n_i = 1.5 x 10¹⁶ m-3, &mu;_e = 0.135 m2 V-1 s-1, and &mu;_h = 0.048 m2 V-1 s-1.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Formula: &sigma; = e n<sub>i</sub> (&mu;<sub>e</sub> + &mu;<sub>h</sub>).<br>&sigma; = (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C)(1.5 &times; 10<sup>16</sup> m<sup>-3</sup>)(0.135 + 0.048) = (2.4 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>)(0.183) = <b>4.392 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> S m<sup>-1</sup> (&Omega;<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-1</sup>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q57: Why cannot a multimeter set to resistance mode measure the exact barrier potential of a p-n junction?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A multimeter internal battery injects an external test current which changes the operating bias of the junction, altering the barrier width. Moreover, internal contact potentials at meter probe junctions cancel the internal barrier.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q58: State the effect of temperature on the conductivity of: (i) a metallic conductor, (ii) a semiconductor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • (i) <b>Metallic Conductor:</b> Conductivity <b>decreases</b> with temperature (&sigma; &prop; 1/T) because increased thermal lattice vibrations scatter conduction electrons, reducing relaxation time &tau;.<br>• (ii) <b>Semiconductor:</b> Conductivity <b>increases exponentially</b> with temperature because thermal energy breaks covalent bonds, exponentially increasing carrier density n<sub>i</sub>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q59: Draw energy band diagrams of: (i) an intrinsic semiconductor, (ii) an n-type semiconductor, (iii) a p-type semiconductor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • <i>Intrinsic:</i> Filled VB, empty CB separated by gap E<sub>g</sub>; Fermi level E<sub>F</sub> is midway in forbidden gap.<br>• <i>n-Type:</i> Donor energy level E<sub>d</sub> is located just below CB (~0.05 eV in Si); Fermi level shifts upwards near CB.<br>• <i>p-Type:</i> Acceptor energy level E<sub>a</sub> is located just above VB (~0.05 eV in Si); Fermi level shifts downwards near VB.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q60: A p-n junction diode has a reverse saturation current of 10 nA at 300 K. What will be its value at 320 K if reverse current doubles for every 10&deg;C rise in temperature?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Temperature rise &Delta;T = 320 - 300 = 20&deg;C (two 10&deg;C increments).<br>Since current doubles every 10&deg;C: I<sub>rev</sub>(320 K) = I<sub>0</sub> &times; 2<sup>(&Delta;T/10)</sup> = 10 nA &times; 2<sup>2</sup> = 10 &times; 4 = <b>40 nA</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q61: Explain why a pure semiconductor behaves as an insulator at absolute zero (0 K).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      At 0 K, thermal kinetic energy is zero (k<sub>B</sub>T = 0). All valence electrons are locked tightly in covalent bonds. The valence band is completely full and conduction band is completely empty with no free carriers available for conduction.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q62: What is the ripple factor of a rectifier? State its value for half-wave and full-wave rectifiers.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Ripple factor (&gamma;) measures the purity of DC output: &gamma; = I<sub>ac</sub> / I<sub>dc</sub> = &radic;[ (I<sub>rms</sub>/I<sub>dc</sub>)<sup>2</sup> - 1 ].<br>• For Half-Wave Rectifier: <b>&gamma; = 1.21</b> (high ripple).<br>• For Full-Wave Rectifier: <b>&gamma; = 0.482</b> (much smoother output).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q63: Explain the term 'Peak Inverse Voltage' (PIV) of a diode in a rectifier circuit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Peak Inverse Voltage (PIV) is the maximum reverse bias voltage that appears across the diode when it is non-conducting (reverse biased).<br>• For Half-Wave Rectifier: <b>PIV = V<sub>m</sub></b>.<br>• For Center-Tapped Full-Wave Rectifier: <b>PIV = 2 V<sub>m</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q64: Why is GaAs (Gallium Arsenide) widely used in optoelectronic devices like LEDs and Solar Cells?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      GaAs is a <b>direct band gap semiconductor (E<sub>g</sub> = 1.42 eV)</b>, where electrons recombine with holes directly with 100% radiative emission of light photons (high quantum efficiency), unlike indirect band gap Si/Ge where energy is lost as lattice heat.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q65: Two identical diodes are connected in parallel in the same forward direction. How does the total forward current compare to a single diode?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The total forward current is <b>doubled</b> because both diodes share the load current equally (I<sub>total</sub> = I<sub>1</sub> + I<sub>2</sub> = 2 I) while maintaining the same forward voltage drop.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q66: Explain how the depletion region acts as a fictitious battery across the p-n junction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The immobile positive donor ions on n-side and negative acceptor ions on p-side create an electric potential difference identical to a small battery with its <b>positive terminal on n-side and negative terminal on p-side</b>, opposing forward current.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q67: A diode is connected across an AC voltage source in series with a resistor. Sketch the waveform of voltage across the resistor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The voltage waveform across the resistor is a <b>pulsating half-wave rectified waveform</b> containing only positive half-cycles (0 to V<sub>m</sub>), while the negative half-cycles are completely clipped to 0 V.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q68: What happens if a diode is connected in reverse bias with a voltage exceeding its breakdown voltage?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The strong reverse electric field causes <b>Zener breakdown or Avalanche breakdown</b>, causing covalent bonds to rupture and reverse current to increase dramatically without any further increase in reverse voltage.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q69: Why is an intrinsic semiconductor doped with only parts-per-million (ppm) levels of impurity atoms?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Adding just 1 ppm impurity increases electrical conductivity by a factor of over <b>100,000 times</b> while preserving the original crystal lattice structure without structural deformation.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q70: Calculate the number of electrons in the conduction band of 1 cm3 of pure silicon at room temperature if n_i = 1.5 x 10¹⁰ cm-3.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Total electrons N = n<sub>i</sub> &times; Volume = (1.5 &times; 10<sup>10</sup> cm<sup>-3</sup>) &times; (1 cm<sup>3</sup>) = <b>1.5 &times; 10<sup>10</sup> electrons</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q71: Why does a full-wave rectifier have twice the efficiency of a half-wave rectifier?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A full-wave rectifier utilizes <b>both positive and negative half cycles</b> of the input AC wave, delivering continuous DC power to the load, whereas a half-wave rectifier wastes the entire negative half cycle.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q72: What is the direction of conventional current inside a forward-biased p-n junction diode?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      From <b>p-side (Anode) to n-side (Cathode)</b> across the junction.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q73: Name a semiconductor that has a negative temperature coefficient of resistance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Silicon (Si) or Germanium (Ge)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q74: Explain the term 'diffusion capacitance' and 'transition capacitance' in a p-n diode.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • <b>Diffusion Capacitance (C<sub>D</sub>):</b> Dominates in forward bias due to storage of injected majority carriers near the junction edges.<br>• <b>Transition (Depletion) Capacitance (C<sub>T</sub>):</b> Dominates in reverse bias due to the dipole charge layer across the widening depletion dielectric.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q75: Calculate the ratio of majority carrier to minority carrier concentration in an n-type silicon sample if n_e = 10²² m-3 and n_i = 1.5 x 10¹⁶ m-3.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub><sup>2</sup> / n<sub>e</sub> = (1.5 &times; 10<sup>16</sup>)<sup>2</sup> / 10<sup>22</sup> = (2.25 &times; 10<sup>32</sup>) / 10<sup>22</sup> = 2.25 &times; 10<sup>10</sup> m<sup>-3</sup>.<br>Ratio: n<sub>e</sub> / n<sub>h</sub> = 10<sup>22</sup> / (2.25 &times; 10<sup>10</sup>) = <b>4.44 &times; 10<sup>11</sup> : 1</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q76: Why is the base region in a junction device made very thin and lightly doped?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A thin, lightly doped region minimizes majority carrier recombination, ensuring nearly 100% of injected carriers pass through to the collector.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q77: State the main difference between drift velocity and thermal velocity of charge carriers in semiconductors.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Thermal velocity is random, omnidirectional, and high (~10<sup>5</sup> m/s) with zero net current; Drift velocity is a net directional motion directed by an applied electric field (~10<sup>-2</sup> to 10<sup>2</sup> m/s) producing measurable current.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q78: Why are p-n junction diodes called non-ohmic circuit elements?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Because their I-V characteristic curve is non-linear and does not obey Ohm's law (I is not directly proportional to V).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q79: What is the effect of illuminating a reverse-biased p-n junction photodiode with light of energy h&nu; &gt; E_g?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Incident photons generate additional electron-hole pairs in the depletion region. The strong reverse electric field sweeps them apart, causing a measurable <b>increase in reverse photocurrent proportional to light intensity</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q80: Why does a light emitting diode (LED) emit light only in forward bias?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In forward bias, injected electrons from n-side recombine with holes from p-side near the junction, releasing the band gap energy E<sub>g</sub> directly as visible photons (h&nu; &approx; E<sub>g</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">SECTION C: Long Answer (LA) Questions (5 Marks Each &bull; Q81 to Q100)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q81: Explain on the basis of energy band theory the classification of solids into conductors, insulators, and semiconductors. Draw labeled energy band diagrams for all three at 0 K and room temperature.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Energy Band Formation:</b> When isolated atoms form a crystal lattice, Pauli's exclusion principle causes overlapping valence electron energy levels to split into continuous bands: Valence Band (VB), Conduction Band (CB), and Forbidden Energy Gap (E<sub>g</sub>).<br>\n      <b>2. Three Classes of Solids:</b><br>\n      • <b>Conductors (Metals):</b> The valence band and conduction band overlap completely (E<sub>g</sub> = 0 eV) or the conduction band is partially filled. A continuous sea of electrons is free to conduct current even at 0 K. Resistivity &rho; ~ 10<sup>-8</sup> to 10<sup>-2</sup> &Omega; m.<br>\n      • <b>Insulators:</b> Have a completely filled valence band and totally empty conduction band separated by a large forbidden gap: <b>E<sub>g</sub> &gt; 3 eV</b> (e.g. Diamond E<sub>g</sub> &approx; 5.4 eV). Even at high temperatures, thermal energy (k<sub>B</sub>T ~ 0.026 eV) cannot excite electrons across the gap. Resistivity &rho; ~ 10<sup>11</sup> to 10<sup>19</sup> &Omega; m.<br>\n      • <b>Semiconductors (Si, Ge):</b> Filled VB and empty CB at 0 K (acting as perfect insulators). They have a small forbidden gap: <b>E<sub>g</sub> &lt; 3 eV</b> (Silicon E<sub>g</sub> = 1.1 eV, Germanium E<sub>g</sub> = 0.7 eV). At room temperature (300 K), thermal energy excites electrons into CB leaving holes in VB, providing moderate conductivity.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q82: Explain with neat diagrams the formation of a p-n junction. Explain the origin of: (i) Diffusion Current, (ii) Drift Current, (iii) Depletion Region, (iv) Barrier Potential. Show that net current is zero at equilibrium.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Concurrent Junction Processes:</b><br>\n      • <b>(i) Diffusion Current:</b> High concentration of holes on p-side and electrons on n-side causes majority carriers to diffuse across the junction interface. This gives rise to a large <b>Diffusion Current (I<sub>diff</sub>) directed from p to n</b>.<br>\n      • <b>(ii) Depletion Region Formation:</b> As electrons cross from n to p, they leave behind uncompensated positive donor ions (N<sub>D</sub><sup>+</sup>) on n-side and neutralize holes, leaving negative acceptor ions (N<sub>A</sub><sup>-</sup>) on p-side. This layer devoid of free mobile carriers is the <b>Depletion Layer</b> (thickness d &approx; 0.5 - 1 &mu;m).<br>\n      • <b>(iii) Internal Electric Field &amp; Drift Current:</b> The uncovered space charge creates a powerful internal electric field &vec;E<sub>i</sub> (~10<sup>5</sup> V/m) directed from n to p. This field sweeps minority carriers across the junction, creating a <b>Drift Current (I<sub>drift</sub>) directed from n to p</b>.<br>\n      • <b>(iv) Barrier Potential (V<sub>0</sub>) &amp; Equilibrium:</b> The electric field creates an electrostatic potential difference V<sub>0</sub> (0.7 V for Si, 0.3 V for Ge). As the barrier grows, I<sub>diff</sub> decreases until it exactly balances drift:<br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>I<sub>net</sub> = I<sub>diffusion</sub> - I<sub>drift</sub> = 0</b></span>.<br>\n      Dynamic equilibrium is established with zero net current in an unbiased p-n junction.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q83: Describe the experimental setup to study the forward and reverse bias I-V characteristics of a p-n junction diode. Explain the physical mechanism of conduction in both biasing modes and define dynamic resistance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Experimental Setup:</b> Consists of a variable DC regulated power supply, a p-n diode, milliammeter (for forward bias) / microammeter (for reverse bias), voltmeter, and current-limiting resistor.<br>\n      <b>2. Forward Biasing Mode (p &rarr; (+), n &rarr; (-)):</b><br>\n      • Applied voltage opposes barrier potential V<sub>0</sub>, reducing effective barrier height to (V<sub>0</sub> - V) and narrowing depletion width.<br>\n      • Below knee voltage (V<sub>k</sub> &approx; 0.7 V for Si), current is negligible.<br>\n      • Beyond V<sub>k</sub>, majority carriers flood across the junction, producing exponential current in milliamperes (mA).<br>\n      <b>3. Reverse Biasing Mode (p &rarr; (-), n &rarr; (+)):</b><br>\n      • Applied voltage reinforces V<sub>0</sub>, increasing barrier height to (V<sub>0</sub> + V) and widening depletion layer.<br>\n      • Majority diffusion is completely blocked; only thermally generated minority carriers drift across, producing a tiny <b>Reverse Saturation Current (I<sub>0</sub> ~ nA for Si, &mu;A for Ge)</b>.<br>\n      <b>4. Dynamic Resistance:</b> <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>r<sub>d</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;V</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;I</span></span></b></span> (Forward r<sub>f</sub> ~ 10 &Omega;; Reverse r<sub>r</sub> ~ 10<sup>6</sup> &Omega;).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q84: With the help of a neat circuit diagram, explain the working of a full-wave center-tapped rectifier. Draw the input and output waveforms. Derive the formula for its rectification efficiency.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Circuit Description:</b> Consists of a center-tapped secondary transformer supplying two diodes D<sub>1</sub> and D<sub>2</sub> connected to load resistor R<sub>L</sub>.<br>\n      <b>2. Working Operation:</b><br>\n      • <i>Positive Half-Cycle:</i> Terminal A is positive and B is negative relative to center tap. Diode D<sub>1</sub> is forward biased (ON) and D<sub>2</sub> is reverse biased (OFF). Current flows from X to Y through R<sub>L</sub>.<br>\n      • <i>Negative Half-Cycle:</i> Terminal A is negative and B is positive. Diode D<sub>2</sub> is forward biased (ON) and D<sub>1</sub> is reverse biased (OFF). Current flows again from X to Y through R<sub>L</sub> in the exact same direction.<br>\n      <b>3. Derivation of Rectification Efficiency (&eta;):</b><br>\n      • Average DC Current: I<sub>dc</sub> = 2 I<sub>m</sub> / &pi; &rArr; Output DC Power P<sub>dc</sub> = I<sub>dc</sub><sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub> = (4 I<sub>m</sub><sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub>) / &pi;<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      • RMS Current: I<sub>rms</sub> = I<sub>m</sub> / &radic;2 &rArr; Input AC Power P<sub>ac</sub> = I<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub> = (I<sub>m</sub><sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub>) / 2.<br>\n      • Efficiency: <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>&eta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sub>dc</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sub>ac</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 / &pi;<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 / 2</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi;<sup>2</sup></span></span> = 81.2%</b></span>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q85: Explain the working of a half-wave rectifier with a labeled circuit diagram and input/output waveforms. Derive the expressions for DC output voltage and rectification efficiency.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Circuit &amp; Operation:</b> Consists of an AC transformer, single diode D, and load resistor R<sub>L</sub>.<br>\n      • <i>Positive Half Cycle:</i> Anode is positive; diode is forward biased and conducts current through R<sub>L</sub>, reproducing input positive half cycle.<br>\n      • <i>Negative Half Cycle:</i> Anode is negative; diode is reverse biased and blocks current (output = 0 V).<br>\n      <b>2. DC Output Voltage:</b><br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>V<sub>dc</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &pi;</span></span> &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>&pi;</sup> V<sub>m</sub> sin(&omega;t) d(&omega;t) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>m</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi;</span></span> &approx; 0.318 V<sub>m</sub></b></span>.<br>\n      <b>3. Rectification Efficiency (&eta;):</b><br>\n      • I<sub>dc</sub> = I<sub>m</sub> / &pi; &rArr; P<sub>dc</sub> = (I<sub>m</sub><sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub>) / &pi;<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      • I<sub>rms</sub> = I<sub>m</sub> / 2 &rArr; P<sub>ac</sub> = (I<sub>m</sub><sup>2</sup> R<sub>L</sub>) / 4.<br>\n      • Efficiency: <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>&eta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sub>dc</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sub>ac</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi;<sup>2</sup></span></span> = 40.6%</b></span>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q86: Explain the difference between n-type and p-type semiconductors on the basis of doping, majority/minority carriers, and energy band diagrams with impurity levels.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Doping Mechanism:</b><br>\n      • <i>n-Type:</i> Doped with Group 15 Pentavalent atoms (P, As, Sb). 4 valence electrons bond covalently with Si; 5th electron is loosely attached (ionization energy ~0.05 eV in Si).<br>\n      • <i>p-Type:</i> Doped with Group 13 Trivalent atoms (B, Al, Ga, In). 3 valence electrons bond with Si, creating an electron vacancy (hole) in the 4th bond.<br>\n      <b>2. Carrier Concentrations:</b><br>\n      • <i>n-Type:</i> Free electrons are majority (n<sub>e</sub> &approx; N<sub>D</sub>), holes are minority (n<sub>h</sub> = n<sub>i</sub><sup>2</sup>/N<sub>D</sub>).<br>\n      • <i>p-Type:</i> Holes are majority (n<sub>h</sub> &approx; N<sub>A</sub>), electrons are minority (n<sub>e</sub> = n<sub>i</sub><sup>2</sup>/N<sub>A</sub>).<br>\n      <b>3. Energy Band Position:</b><br>\n      • <i>n-Type:</i> Donor energy level E<sub>d</sub> lies ~0.05 eV below the bottom of Conduction Band.<br>\n      • <i>p-Type:</i> Acceptor energy level E<sub>a</sub> lies ~0.05 eV above the top of Valence Band.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q87: What is a filter circuit? Explain the action of a capacitor filter in reducing ripple in a full-wave rectifier circuit with neat waveforms.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Purpose of Filter:</b> The output of a rectifier is pulsating DC containing AC ripple components. A filter circuit removes AC ripples to deliver steady, smooth DC voltage.<br>\n      <b>2. Working of Shunt Capacitor Filter (C):</b><br>\n      • A large capacitor C is connected in parallel with load resistor R<sub>L</sub>.<br>\n      • As rectifier output rises to peak V<sub>m</sub>, capacitor charges rapidly through the low forward resistance of the diode.<br>\n      • When rectifier output falls from V<sub>m</sub> to zero, the diode turns OFF. The capacitor now discharges slowly through load R<sub>L</sub>.<br>\n      • If time constant &tau; = R<sub>L</sub>C is large (&tau; &gt;&gt; T/2), the capacitor voltage decays only slightly before the next pulse charges it again to V<sub>m</sub>.<br>\n      • <i>Result:</i> Peak-to-peak ripple is drastically suppressed: <b>V<sub>ripple</sub> &approx; I<sub>dc</sub> / (2 f C)</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q88: Explain why the conductivity of an intrinsic semiconductor increases with temperature whereas that of a metallic conductor decreases.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Mathematical Conductivity Relation:</b> &sigma; = n e &mu; = n e<sup>2</sup> &tau; / m.<br>\n      <b>2. Case of Metallic Conductors:</b><br>\n      • Free electron density n is extremely high (~10<sup>28</sup> m<sup>-3</sup>) and constant.<br>\n      • As temperature rises, thermal lattice vibrations increase, causing more frequent collisions and reducing mean relaxation time &tau;.<br>\n      • Since &tau; decreases while n is unchanged, <b>conductivity &sigma; decreases (resistivity increases)</b>.<br>\n      <b>3. Case of Semiconductors:</b><br>\n      • Carrier density is given by: n<sub>i</sub>(T) &prop; T<sup>3/2</sup> exp(-E<sub>g</sub> / 2k<sub>B</sub>T).<br>\n      • As temperature rises, thermal breaking of covalent bonds increases n<sub>e</sub> and n<sub>h</sub> <i>exponentially</i>.<br>\n      • This massive exponential rise in carrier density overwhelming surpasses the small decrease in &tau;, causing <b>conductivity &sigma; to increase exponentially</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q89: A full-wave bridge rectifier is supplied with 230 V AC at 50 Hz through a 10:1 step-down transformer. Calculate: (a) DC output voltage, (b) PIV across each diode, (c) output ripple frequency.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      Given: V<sub>primary</sub> = 230 V, Ratio = 10:1 &rArr; V<sub>rms</sub> = 230 / 10 = 23 V.<br>\n      Peak secondary voltage: V<sub>m</sub> = &radic;2 &times; 23 V = 1.414 &times; 23 = <b>32.52 V</b>.<br>\n      <b>(a) DC Output Voltage:</b><br>\n      V<sub>dc</sub> = 2 V<sub>m</sub> / &pi; = (2 &times; 32.52) / 3.1416 = 65.04 / 3.1416 = <b>20.70 V</b>.<br>\n      <b>(b) Peak Inverse Voltage (PIV) per diode:</b><br>\n      For a Bridge Rectifier: <b>PIV = V<sub>m</sub> = 32.52 V</b>.<br>\n      <b>(c) Output Ripple Frequency:</b><br>\n      f<sub>out</sub> = 2 &times; f<sub>in</sub> = 2 &times; 50 Hz = <b>100 Hz</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q90: Explain why a semiconductor diode is non-linear and non-ohmic. How is dynamic resistance calculated from its experimental I-V characteristic curve?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Non-Ohmic &amp; Non-Linear Behavior:</b><br>\n      • A device is ohmic if current is strictly proportional to voltage (I &prop; V, straight line through origin).<br>\n      • In a p-n diode, forward current is exponential: I = I<sub>0</sub> [exp(e V / &eta; k<sub>B</sub> T) - 1].<br>\n      • In reverse bias, current saturates to -I<sub>0</sub> and does not depend on voltage.<br>\n      • The ratio V/I is not constant; hence, it cannot be characterized by a single static resistance.<br>\n      <b>2. Dynamic Resistance Calculation:</b><br>\n      • Select an operating point Q(V<sub>1</sub>, I<sub>1</sub>) on the characteristic curve.<br>\n      • Apply a small voltage perturbation &Delta;V and measure the resulting current change &Delta;I.<br>\n      • Dynamic resistance is the reciprocal of the slope of tangent at Q: <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>r<sub>d</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;V</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;I</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Slope</span></span></b></span>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q91: Explain the term 'Barrier Potential' in a p-n junction diode. Discuss the factors affecting its value and explain why it decreases with increasing temperature.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Physical Origin:</b> Barrier potential V<sub>0</sub> is the electrostatic potential difference established across the depletion layer by the uncompensated positive donor and negative acceptor ions, opposing further majority carrier diffusion.<br>\n      <b>2. Factors Affecting Barrier Potential:</b><br>\n      • <i>Semiconductor Material:</i> Silicon (E<sub>g</sub> = 1.1 eV &rArr; V<sub>0</sub> &approx; 0.7 V); Germanium (E<sub>g</sub> = 0.7 eV &rArr; V<sub>0</sub> &approx; 0.3 V).<br>\n      • <i>Doping Density:</i> Higher doping increases space charge density and narrows depletion width, increasing V<sub>0</sub>: V<sub>0</sub> = (k<sub>B</sub>T/e) ln(N<sub>A</sub> N<sub>D</sub> / n<sub>i</sub><sup>2</sup>).<br>\n      • <i>Temperature:</i> As temperature rises, intrinsic carrier concentration n<sub>i</sub> increases exponentially, reducing the logarithmic ratio and narrowing the band gap, causing V<sub>0</sub> to <b>decrease at ~2 mV / &deg;C</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q92: Describe the construction and working of a p-n junction Photodiode. Explain why it is operated in reverse bias mode.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Construction &amp; Working:</b><br>\n      A photodiode is a reverse-biased p-n junction packaged with a transparent glass/quartz window over the depletion region.<br>\n      When photons of energy h&nu; &gt; E<sub>g</sub> strike the depletion layer, they are absorbed, breaking covalent bonds and generating electron-hole pairs.<br>\n      The high built-in electric field rapidly sweeps electrons to n-side and holes to p-side before recombination, producing a reverse photocurrent I<sub>photo</sub>.<br>\n      <b>2. Reason for Operating in Reverse Bias:</b><br>\n      In reverse bias, dark current is extremely small (I<sub>0</sub> ~ nA). The photo-generated carrier current &Delta;I produces a <b>huge fractional change (&Delta;I / I<sub>0</sub>)</b> that is easily detected and directly proportional to light intensity. In forward bias, initial current is already large (mA), making the fractional change negligible.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q93: Explain the construction, working, and characteristic curve of a Light Emitting Diode (LED). What are its advantages over incandescent lamps?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Working of LED:</b> An LED is a heavily doped p-n junction fabricated from direct band gap compound semiconductors (e.g. GaAsP, GaN) operated in <b>Forward Bias</b>.<br>\n      • Forward bias injects majority electrons and holes across the junction.<br>\n      • Near the junction boundary, electrons fall from conduction band into valence band holes, recombining and releasing the energy gap directly as visible photons: <b>h&nu; &approx; E<sub>g</sub></b>.<br>\n      <b>2. Key Advantages over Incandescent Bulbs:</b><br>\n      • <i>Energy Efficiency:</i> Converts electricity directly to light with almost zero heat loss (~80% more efficient).<br>\n      • <i>Long Operating Life:</i> Operates for &gt;50,000 hours (50&times; incandescent lamps).<br>\n      • <i>Fast Switching Speed:</i> Nanosecond response time (&Delta;t &lt; 10<sup>-9</sup> s).<br>\n      • <i>Low Operating Voltage:</i> Operates on safe low DC voltages (~1.8 - 3.3 V).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q94: Explain the construction and working of a Solar Cell. Draw its I-V characteristic curve and explain why it is drawn in the fourth quadrant.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Construction &amp; Working:</b> A solar cell is a large-area p-n junction with an ultra-thin top p-layer (~0.3 &mu;m) exposed to sunlight via finger electrodes.<br>\n      • <i>1. Generation:</i> Sunlight (h&nu; &gt; E<sub>g</sub>) penetrates near the junction, generating electron-hole pairs.<br>\n      • <i>2. Separation:</i> Built-in electric field &vec;E<sub>i</sub> sweeps electrons to n-side and holes to p-side.<br>\n      • <i>3. Collection:</i> Accumulation of charges creates a photovoltage (Open-Circuit Voltage V<sub>oc</sub> ~ 0.6 V) across external terminals.<br>\n      <b>2. Why Fourth Quadrant?:</b> A solar cell does not consume electrical power from a circuit; instead, it <b>delivers power to an external load (P = V &times; I &lt; 0)</b>. Current flows out of positive terminal, placing the I-V curve in the IV quadrant.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q95: A p-type semiconductor has an acceptor level 0.05 eV above the valence band. What is the maximum wavelength of light that can excite an electron from the valence band to the acceptor level?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      Given: &Delta;E = 0.05 eV = 0.05 &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> J = 8.0 &times; 10<sup>-21</sup> J.<br>\n      Formula: &Delta;E = h c / &lambda;<sub>max</sub> &rArr; &lambda;<sub>max</sub> = h c / &Delta;E.<br>\n      &lambda;<sub>max</sub> = (6.63 &times; 10<sup>-34</sup> &times; 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (8.0 &times; 10<sup>-21</sup>) = (1.989 &times; 10<sup>-25</sup>) / (8.0 &times; 10<sup>-21</sup>) = <b>2.486 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup> m = 24.86 &mu;m</b> (Far Infrared).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q96: Explain why a Zener diode is heavily doped and describe the mechanism of Zener breakdown.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Heavy Doping Feature:</b> A Zener diode is doped with heavy impurity concentration (~1 in 10<sup>3</sup> atoms).<br>\n      • Heavy doping results in an <b>ultra-thin depletion layer width (d &lt; 10 nm = 10<sup>-8</sup> m)</b>.<br>\n      <b>2. Zener Breakdown Mechanism:</b><br>\n      • Applying a modest reverse voltage (~4 - 6 V) produces an enormous internal electric field across the narrow depletion layer: <b>E = V / d &approx; 6 V / 10<sup>-8</sup> m = 6 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> V/m</b>.<br>\n      • This colossal field exerts immense force on valence electrons, pulling them directly out of covalent bonds into the conduction band by quantum mechanical tunneling (field ionization), causing sharp current breakdown at a precise voltage V<sub>z</sub>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q97: Explain the working of a Zener diode as a DC Voltage Regulator with a neat circuit diagram.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Circuit Description:</b> The unregulated DC input voltage V<sub>in</sub> (&gt; V<sub>z</sub>) is connected to a reverse-biased Zener diode in parallel with load resistor R<sub>L</sub>, through a series current-limiting resistor R<sub>s</sub>.<br>\n      <b>2. Voltage Regulation Action:</b><br>\n      • When input voltage V<sub>in</sub> increases, diode operates in Zener breakdown region where voltage across it remains fixed at <b>V<sub>out</sub> = V<sub>z</sub></b>.<br>\n      • The excess voltage (V<sub>in</sub> - V<sub>z</sub>) drops entirely across series resistor R<sub>s</sub>: I<sub>s</sub> = (V<sub>in</sub> - V<sub>z</sub>) / R<sub>s</sub>.<br>\n      • The Zener diode absorbs the extra current &Delta;I<sub>z</sub> while load current I<sub>L</sub> = V<sub>z</sub>/R<sub>L</sub> and output voltage V<sub>out</sub> remain <b>strictly constant</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q98: Calculate the value of series resistance R_s required to regulate a 12 V DC supply to a 6 V output using a Zener diode of maximum power rating P_z = 300 mW with load current I_L = 20 mA.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      Given: V<sub>in</sub> = 12 V, V<sub>z</sub> = 6 V, I<sub>L</sub> = 20 mA = 0.020 A, P<sub>z(max)</sub> = 300 mW = 0.30 W.<br>\n      Maximum Zener current: I<sub>z(max)</sub> = P<sub>z</sub> / V<sub>z</sub> = 0.30 / 6 = 0.050 A = 50 mA.<br>\n      Total circuit current I = I<sub>z</sub> + I<sub>L</sub> = 50 mA + 20 mA = 70 mA = 0.070 A.<br>\n      Voltage drop across series resistor: V<sub>Rs</sub> = V<sub>in</sub> - V<sub>z</sub> = 12 - 6 = 6 V.<br>\n      Series resistance: <b>R<sub>s</sub> = V<sub>Rs</sub> / I = 6 / 0.070 = 85.7 &Omega; &approx; 86 &Omega;</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q99: State the differences between Avalanche breakdown and Zener breakdown in semiconductor diodes.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>Comprehensive Breakdown Comparison:</b><br>\n      • <i>1. Doping Level:</i> Zener breakdown occurs in heavily doped diodes; Avalanche breakdown occurs in lightly/moderately doped diodes.<br>\n      • <i>2. Depletion Width:</i> Zener occurs across thin depletion layer (d &lt; 10 nm); Avalanche occurs across wide depletion layer.<br>\n      • <i>3. Breakdown Voltage:</i> Zener occurs at low reverse voltages (V<sub>z</sub> &lt; 6 V); Avalanche occurs at higher reverse voltages (V<sub>br</sub> &gt; 6 V).<br>\n      • <i>4. Mechanism:</i> Zener is direct rupture of covalent bonds by intense electric field (field ionization); Avalanche is cumulative collision impact ionization by accelerated minority carriers.<br>\n      • <i>5. Temperature Coefficient:</i> Zener has negative temperature coefficient (&alpha; &lt; 0); Avalanche has positive temperature coefficient (&alpha; &gt; 0).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q100: Explain why a bridge rectifier is widely preferred over a center-tapped full-wave rectifier in practical power supply design.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>Key Advantages of Bridge Rectifier:</b><br>\n      • <b>1. No Bulky Center-Tapped Transformer:</b> A bridge rectifier operates with a simple, standard secondary transformer, reducing cost, weight, and volume.<br>\n      • <b>2. Lower Peak Inverse Voltage (PIV):</b> In a bridge rectifier, PIV across each diode is only <b>V<sub>m</sub></b> (compared to <b>2 V<sub>m</sub></b> in center-tapped), allowing use of lower-voltage, cheaper diodes.<br>\n      • <b>3. Higher Transformer Utilization Factor (TUF):</b> The secondary winding conducts continuously during both half cycles, maximizing efficiency and transformer utilization.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

